WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.883
APIs need explicit authentication and authorization rules.

2
00:00:03.883 --> 00:00:21.689
Whether the application uses controllers, Minimal APIs, endpoint groups, or a mix, reviewers should be able to see which endpoints are public, which require an authenticated caller, which require a policy, and which are intended only for internal or operational use.

3
00:00:21.689 --> 00:00:27.312
Security rules should not be hidden in assumptions about routes or network location.

4
00:00:27.312 --> 00:00:32.065
Minimal APIs and endpoint groups can be organized so rules are visible.

5
00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:42.441
Grouping related endpoints, applying shared authorization metadata, and keeping high-risk operations separate from public endpoints can make review easier.

6
00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:51.211
Controllers and Razor Pages benefit from the same discipline: consistent attributes, clear policy names, and documented exceptions.

7
00:00:51.211 --> 00:00:56.165
DTOs help prevent over-posting and accidental binding of sensitive fields.

8
00:00:56.165 --> 00:01:02.457
A request type should represent the fields the caller is allowed to supply for that operation.

9
00:01:02.457 --> 00:01:15.779
It should not expose internal flags, tenant ownership, approval status, pricing decisions, administrative fields, or server-controlled properties simply because those fields exist on a domain object.

10
00:01:15.779 --> 00:01:18.523
Responses should be shaped intentionally.

11
00:01:18.523 --> 00:01:31.041
Return the data the caller needs, use consistent error handling, avoid internal exception detail, and choose status codes that reflect the outcome without revealing unnecessary internals.

12
00:01:31.041 --> 00:01:37.133
A safe API contract is stable, minimal, and understandable to both consumers and reviewers.

13
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:42.355
Rate-limit thinking and abuse resistance belong in API design at a high level.

14
00:01:42.355 --> 00:01:50.589
Teams should consider expensive operations, repeated failures, large requests, dependency pressure, and operational limits.

15
00:01:50.589 --> 00:02:00.362
The exact implementation may vary, but the application should not assume every client behaves politely or every dependency has unlimited capacity.

16
00:02:00.362 --> 00:02:03.910
Service-to-service trust should be handled carefully.

17
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.521
Network location alone is not identity.

18
00:02:06.521 --> 00:02:17.432
A request from an internal network, private subnet, service mesh, or known host still needs a clear identity and authorization model appropriate for the operation.

19
00:02:17.432 --> 00:02:23.189
Internal APIs often handle sensitive data and deserve the same clarity as public APIs.
